The following alternative notations are also widely used to represent second-order partial derivatives:

$$
\begin{aligned}\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{x}^{2}}(f) & =\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}={f}_{xx}\,\,\,\,\,\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{y}^{2}}(f)=\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}={f}_{yy} \\ \frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂y\,∂x}(f) & =\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y\,∂x}={f}_{xy}\,\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x\,∂y}(f)=\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x\,∂y}={f}_{yx}\end{aligned}
$$

**Note:** In our example, the two mixed partial derivatives were equal:

$$
f_{xy}(x,y) = 2y + xye^{xy} + e^{xy} = f_{yx}(x,y)
$$

Later, we will see that under rather mild conditions on $f,$ we almost always have that $f_{xy}=f_{yx}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "feb54aa0-17fb-5468-8b99-14174a6bd059" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2397 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id 17592186125270 :step/content {:db/id 17592186124440 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186124244 :question/id #uuid "0a8f5141-941d-5059-9d9f-bf9e5b09096f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3127"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ea31275-77bd-59c7-8e1d-96e2e4b252a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048707} {:db/id 17592186050902}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186124935} {:db/id 17592186125185} {:db/id 17592186235546} {:db/id 17592186268915} {:db/id 17592186268916} {:db/id 17592186335782} {:db/id 17592186335784} {:db/id 17592186348377} {:db/id 17592186382138} {:db/id 17592186382358} {:db/id 17592186382365} {:db/id 17592186382372}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Mixed Second-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "4f4f64aa-ab4f-5abb-8d9c-ca2aa2790b64" :step/math-academy-id 10559 :step/next {:db/id 17592186125271}} {:db/id 17592186125271 :step/content {:db/id 17592186124441 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186124245 :question/id #uuid "e16f6686-dffb-554e-a50a-8760e818439f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3129"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a0bfe-7c69-5ad0-ad1d-aa8692dae673" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186124932} {:db/id 17592186124946} {:db/id 17592186268918} {:db/id 17592186268919} {:db/id 17592186268920} {:db/id 17592186268921} {:db/id 17592186348378} {:db/id 17592186348384} {:db/id 17592186348391} {:db/id 17592186348392} {:db/id 17592186382379} {:db/id 17592186382386}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Expressions Involving Second-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "a25341f7-8d06-561b-9802-42d2f9c792e8" :step/math-academy-id 10563 :step/next {:db/id 17592186125272}} {:db/id 17592186125272 :step/content {:db/id 17592186125725 :tutorial/content "We can also define other **higher order partial derivatives** such as $f_{xxy}, \, f_{xxyy}, \, f_{xyxy},$ etc. 

For instance, the function $f(x,y,z)= z\cos(xy)$ has the first-order partial derivative

$$
f_y(x,y,z) = -xz \sin(xy)
$$

and the second-order partial derivative

$$
f_{yx}(x,y,z)= -x yz \cos{\left(x y \right)} - z\sin{\left(x y \right)}.
$$

If we differentiate again, with respect to $x,$ then we get the **third-order partial derivative** $f_{yxx}\mathbin{:}$ 

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxx}(x,y,z) & =\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yx}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(-xyz\operatorname{cos}(xy)-z\operatorname{sin}(xy)) \\  & =-yz\operatorname{cos}(xy)-xyz(-\operatorname{sin}(xy)\cdot y)-z\operatorname{cos}(xy)\cdot y \\  & =x{y}^{2}z\operatorname{sin}(xy)-2yz\operatorname{cos}(xy)\end{aligned}
$$

In general, to compute a $n$th-order partial derivative, we need to compute a sequence of $n$ individual derivatives." :tutorial/id #uuid "e256986d-ca95-50b3-a16c-0f46005b5c66" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2398 :tutorial/title "Higher-Order Partial Derivatives for Functions of  More Than Two Variables"} :step/id #uuid "f8b4cf47-e81c-516c-8eef-7d544743362a" :step/math-academy-id 10566 :step/next {:db/id 17592186125273}} {:db/id 17592186125725 :tutorial/content "We can also define other **higher order partial derivatives** such as $f_{xxy}, \, f_{xxyy}, \, f_{xyxy},$ etc. 

For instance, the function $f(x,y,z)= z\cos(xy)$ has the first-order partial derivative

$$
f_y(x,y,z) = -xz \sin(xy)
$$

and the second-order partial derivative

$$
f_{yx}(x,y,z)= -x yz \cos{\left(x y \right)} - z\sin{\left(x y \right)}.
$$

If we differentiate again, with respect to $x,$ then we get the **third-order partial derivative** $f_{yxx}\mathbin{:}$ 

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxx}(x,y,z) & =\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yx}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(-xyz\operatorname{cos}(xy)-z\operatorname{sin}(xy)) \\  & =-yz\operatorname{cos}(xy)-xyz(-\operatorname{sin}(xy)\cdot y)-z\operatorname{cos}(xy)\cdot y \\  & =x{y}^{2}z\operatorname{sin}(xy)-2yz\operatorname{cos}(xy)\end{aligned}
$$

In general, to compute a $n$th-order partial derivative, we need to compute a sequence of $n$ individual derivatives." :tutorial/id #uuid "e256986d-ca95-50b3-a16c-0f46005b5c66" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2398 :tutorial/title "Higher-Order Partial Derivatives for Functions of  More Than Two Variables"} {:db/id 17592186125273 :step/content {:db/id 17592186124442 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186124246 :question/id #uuid "a41bfb06-4372-5124-b910-4b35f19d148b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3130"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83724b66-0f15-5b94-9576-76e9dfd56d38" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186124939} {:db/id 17592186124948} {:db/id 17592186268922} {:db/id 17592186268923} {:db/id 17592186268924} {:db/id 17592186335787} {:db/id 17592186335788} {:db/id 17592186348393} {:db/id 17592186381062} {:db/id 17592186381069} {:db/id 17592186382131} {:db/id 17592186422010} {:db/id 17592186422011}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Higher-Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "25a77626-7a96-5d1b-b087-a01012cbeb91" :step/math-academy-id 10567} {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} {:activity/course {:db/id 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186422014} :activity/id #uuid "59407835-7bfb-56b8-b3fd-56d3687982eb" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186048781 :topic/id #uuid "be3a8d83-d961-5a49-82b6-e2c59f1bcd4b" :topic/math-academy-id 1933} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186422012 :step/content {:db/id 17592186422011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c70e303-c80e-51d2-8821-2af0cf8b3950" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})-x\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id 17592186421995} {:answer/id #uuid "92245356-2a0e-55cd-8484-17313be9c3ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})-2x\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id 17592186422003} {:answer/id #uuid "992b5993-493b-561e-9033-5437b3de59e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id 17592186422004} {:answer/id #uuid "ca872c1a-6f89-5f8b-8336-d7cb854e1bcb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x{y}^{2}\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id 17592186422005} {:answer/id #uuid "d1afdc0c-1cf1-54cf-9c95-d81dfb59dc49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id 17592186422006}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186422006} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a95cb7d-93f6-5084-ade9-1c85a3b6b1f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186422008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fdce2d33-30de-52e0-89c4-590147a8c4ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-80479" :question/problem "Calculate ${g}_{xxy}(x,y)$ given that $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y):$ 

$$
\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(({x}^{2}+y)\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})) \\  & =2x\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\end{aligned}
$$

Next, we find ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$

$$
\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})) \\  & =2\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\end{aligned}
$$

Finally, we find ${g}_{xxy}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ 

$$
\begin{aligned}{g}_{xxy}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{xx}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})) \\  & =4y\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\end{aligned}
$$"} :step/id #uuid "25f65be6-b106-53d1-9640-27a18badcd80" :step/next {:db/id 17592186422015}} {:db/id 17592186422013 :step/content {:db/id 17592186422010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b7ad2e-54cc-50e3-9ca3-0b8efd17be6d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y+{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186421994} {:answer/id #uuid "1f109dc7-15d8-584f-8e2b-595762c1a061" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "4xy+y{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186421996} {:answer/id #uuid "44455996-432d-5a3d-8b99-8ba088ea40bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy+{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186421998} {:answer/id #uuid "5e886d04-bc8d-58c5-9141-d4629aa452fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}+y{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186421999} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c5be38-2517-52b4-89a2-e748dfb3f71f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "y+x+{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186422007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186421994} :answer-field/id #uuid "31dfaa29-640a-5223-927a-6d7e182b7cd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186422009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "11508c24-c4aa-589a-b2fa-c4b12396bfe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-65070" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}_{xyx}(x,y)$ given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y{e}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y):$ 

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y{e}^{x}) \\  & =2x\cdot {y}^{2}+y\cdot {e}^{x} \\  & =2x{y}^{2}+y{e}^{x}\end{aligned}
$$

Next, we find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}+y{e}^{x}) \\  & =2x\cdot 2y+1\cdot {e}^{x} \\  & =4xy+{e}^{x}\end{aligned}
$$

Finally, we find ${f}_{xyx}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$ 

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyx}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+{e}^{x}) \\  & =4\cdot 1\cdot y+{e}^{x} \\  & =4y+{e}^{x}\end{aligned}
$$"} :step/id #uuid "4932cd51-46e5-51d8-99f7-f0e3336e3476" :step/next {:db/id 17592186422012}} {:db/id 17592186422014 :step/content {:db/id 17592186124932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b1340c-9ea7-510e-a7fe-a5318d1aa165" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id 17592186421993} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea483d9-4903-536f-a106-b11346fafb16" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id 17592186421997} {:answer/id #uuid "646a4289-2437-5a76-800d-038e82b06562" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\operatorname{sin}⁡y)" :db/id 17592186422000} {:answer/id #uuid "654452d8-a947-5e7b-b22c-8a13e2ef81d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x(\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\operatorname{sin}⁡y)" :db/id 17592186422001} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0fbde1-6b7d-56c5-b32e-24ed9e9df6a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id 17592186422002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186421997} :answer-field/id #uuid "f436de7c-3f9a-5c8c-8ef3-8a7a55ee418d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186122192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9fe7b82e-efd4-53ad-afa8-cb700e6400e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-35936" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y)$ given that  ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\  & =2x\operatorname{cos}⁡y\end{aligned}
$$

To find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\  & =2x\operatorname{cos}⁡y\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =2x\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2x\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\  & =4x\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\end{aligned}
$$"} :step/id #uuid "73d31843-2db2-5471-8b39-85a8ef5439bb" :step/next {:db/id 17592186422016}} {:db/id 17592186422015 :step/content {:db/id 17592186268920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "573ca17f-8b55-50aa-b136-59bd043a6536" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\sqrt{3}-\pi " :db/id 17592186266921} {:answer/id #uuid "5df633bf-d9fe-5623-9aed-bd0cc78f2d1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186266974} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc7ddd0-7d66-560f-b925-d71f0a4f1da7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{2}(\sqrt{3}+\pi )" :db/id 17592186266989} {:answer/id #uuid "75e2d132-79e6-565d-b0a0-3480a84f1042" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{2}(\pi -\sqrt{3})" :db/id 17592186267184} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6cc2bf-6cc9-5989-82ab-f94ca56c41d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{1}{2}(\sqrt{3}-\frac{\pi }{3})" :db/id 17592186267685}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186266974} :answer-field/id #uuid "0722938f-1bbb-5341-b742-6808aa83c68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186268418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3aaf83dc-c135-54d5-b395-8dc2f637ad0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132894" :question/problem "Find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)-{g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(\frac{\pi }{3},\frac{\pi }{3})$ given that ${g}_{x}(x,y)=x\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ and ${g}_{y}(x,y)=x\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y).$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$

$$
\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)) \\  & =\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\  & =2\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\end{aligned}
$$



To find ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$

$$
\begin{aligned}{g}_{yy}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{y}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)) \\  & =x\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\end{aligned}
$$



Next, we evaluate ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ and ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(\frac{\pi }{3},\frac{\pi }{3}):$

$$
\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(\frac{\pi }{3},\frac{\pi }{3}) & =2\operatorname{sin}⁡(\frac{\pi }{3}+\frac{\pi }{3})+\frac{\pi }{3}\operatorname{cos}⁡(\frac{\pi }{3}+\frac{\pi }{3})=\sqrt{3}-\frac{\pi }{6} \\ {g}_{yy}(\frac{\pi }{3},\frac{\pi }{3}) & =\frac{\pi }{3}\operatorname{cos}⁡(\frac{\pi }{3}+\frac{\pi }{3})=-\frac{\pi }{6}\end{aligned}
$$

Finally, we have

$$
{g}_{xx}(\frac{\pi }{3},\frac{\pi }{3})-{g}_{yy}(\frac{\pi }{3},\frac{\pi }{3})=\sqrt{3}-\frac{\pi }{6}-(-\frac{\pi }{6})=\sqrt{3}.
$$"} :step/id #uuid "999e1519-06f2-527e-a6e9-193c3c031bfa"} {:db/id 17592186422016 :step/content {:db/id 17592186124939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9137d290-41b5-56f3-91a0-1bd21a361f51" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id 17592186122235} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8c61f9-4828-5963-94b5-9c4024267fa6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186122236} {:answer/id #uuid "fb781ced-0c22-5194-a9bf-5a48f6b0b287" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{2}{y}" :db/id 17592186122237} {:answer/id #uuid "54475861-eeaf-5969-a8d8-c01e85facc65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186122238} {:answer/id #uuid "74a0e08c-a99c-5a0d-80ac-df9b1bbcc42b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "\frac{2}{x}" :db/id 17592186122239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186122236} :answer-field/id #uuid "0485544a-7efe-5a7c-a375-d04fc25e6208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186122234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca6dfc54-54dd-5d46-99aa-e23ca5426a6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-35969" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}_{xyx}(x,y)$ given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y):$

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\  & =2xy\end{aligned}
$$

Next, we find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy) \\  & =2x\end{aligned}
$$

Finally, we find ${f}_{xyx}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$ 

$$
\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyx}(x,y) & =\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\  & =\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x) \\  & =2\end{aligned}
